<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">What is a website migration?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading --></div>
<!-- /wp:column -->

<!-- wp:column {"width":"60%"} -->
<div class="wp-block-column" style="flex-basis:60%"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A migration is any big change to where or how your website lives: a new host, a new platform, a new design or a new domain name. From a visitor's side the site may look better afterwards. From Google's side, it can look like a different site.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The danger is in the addresses. Google remembers each page by its address. If "/services/boiler-repair" becomes "/boiler-repairs/" and nothing points the old one to the new one, that page starts again from nothing, and so does every link other sites made to it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:details {"className":"ifm-readmore"} -->
<details class="wp-block-details ifm-readmore"><summary>Read more</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A safe move has three parts. Before: list every existing address and what brings visitors to it. During: set up a redirect from each old address to its closest new page, and carry over titles and content. After: watch Search Console for errors and fix them quickly.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
<!-- /wp:column --></div>
<!-- /wp:columns --></section>
<!-- /wp:group -->

